\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}The multi-object addendum: five falsified predictions, one mechanism retracted, and a fourth memorization layer}{3}{section.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces How the third object crossed. \textbf  {(A)} Appearance drift by episode tick: the blocked object's deployment embeddings sit furthest from its own training corpus \emph  {at the start}, before the arm has acted on any prediction, and the gap shrinks 65\% --- the opposite of the rising trend the two crossing objects show, which is what a \emph  {consequence} of a bad goal looks like. This is the circularity check that retired two earlier instruments. \textbf  {(B)} The miss decomposed: 94\% lateral, with the blocked object's \emph  {height} better than the crossing object's, which rules out a descent failure. \textbf  {(C)} The intervention, $n{=}50$ per arm on identical seeds, Wilson 95\% intervals: fine-tuning the grasp head on the machine's own viewpoints takes the blocked object from 3/50 to 18/50 (paired exact McNemar $p{=}6.1\times 10^{-5}$, 15/15 discordant one-way), against the crossing object's 35/50 for reference.}}{5}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:third}{{2}{5}{How the third object crossed. \textbf {(A)} Appearance drift by episode tick: the blocked object's deployment embeddings sit furthest from its own training corpus \emph {at the start}, before the arm has acted on any prediction, and the gap shrinks 65\% --- the opposite of the rising trend the two crossing objects show, which is what a \emph {consequence} of a bad goal looks like. This is the circularity check that retired two earlier instruments. \textbf {(B)} The miss decomposed: 94\% lateral, with the blocked object's \emph {height} better than the crossing object's, which rules out a descent failure. \textbf {(C)} The intervention, $n{=}50$ per arm on identical seeds, Wilson 95\% intervals: fine-tuning the grasp head on the machine's own viewpoints takes the blocked object from 3/50 to 18/50 (paired exact McNemar $p{=}6.1\times 10^{-5}$, 15/15 discordant one-way), against the crossing object's 35/50 for reference}{figure.caption.5}{}}
